        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "HtmlFormat"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "diagram": {
            "x": 8315.267679837232,
            "y": 8590.605929741998
          },
          "code": "HTMLFORMAT",
          "convergenceTargetId": "1774392700334",
          "id": "1774246149898",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1774246149901_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": " GOAL:\n  Convert the selected input data into properly structured, valid HTML while preserving the factual content and intended meaning.\n\n  ==================================================\n  INPUT DATA:\n  Format the following input data as HTML:\n  {{$context.$nodes.RETURN_RAW_RESPONSE.$output.result}}\n\n  ==================================================\n  HTML RENDERING STRICT RULES (CRITICAL)\n\n  - Return RAW HTML ONLY.\n  - NEVER escape quotes inside HTML.\n  - Use normal double quotes only.\n  - The entire response MUST be a SINGLE unified HTML block.\n  - Do NOT mix HTML and plain text rendering.\n  - Ensure ALL output is inside valid HTML tags.\n  - Do NOT wrap HTML inside JSON strings or any encoded structure.\n\n  ==================================================\n  ABSOLUTE PRIORITY ORDER\n\n  1. Return raw valid HTML only.\n  2. Never render communication-style content as tables.\n  3. For table-eligible non-communication records, render table(s) first.\n  4. Render narrative sections after tables.\n  5. Apply heading/list/metric formatting cleanup.\n\n  ==================================================\n  COMMUNICATION CONTENT RULE (HIGH PRIORITY)\n\n  - Do NOT use HTML tables for Emails, Email threads, Phone numbers, Phone, Calls, Conversations, Chat messages, Notes, or any narrative communication content.\n  - Those must always be rendered using <p>, <ul>, <ol>, and <li> only.\n  - Even if multiple communication records exist, do NOT convert them into a table.\n  - Preserve conversational and narrative readability for communication-style content.\n  - If communication content contains multiple distinct items, render each item on its own new line using separate <p> tags or separate <li> items.\n  - Never compress multiple emails, calls, notes, or conversation points into one dense paragraph.\n  - Never render communication content as raw key-value lines or inline numbered text.\n\n  ==================================================\n  RESPONSE STRUCTURE PRIORITY (HIGHEST → LOWEST)\n\n  1. TABLE RULE (HIGHEST PRIORITY)\n  - If the selected input contains tabular or record-style data for any of the following and has more than one row, render it as an HTML table:\n    * Opportunity\n    * Lead\n    * Asset\n    * Subscription\n    * Invoice\n    * Account Hierarchy\n    * CRMServiceRequest\n    * List of Contacts\n\n  2. STRUCTURE PRESERVATION RULE\n  - If input contains bullet points or lists, preserve them using <ul>/<ol>/<li>.\n\n  3. DEFAULT PARAGRAPH RULE\n  - Use paragraph format only when the input is natural narrative or a direct short answer.\n\n  ==================================================\n  TABLE-FIRST LAYOUT RULE (HIGHEST PRIORITY)\n\n  - If the selected input contains both:\n    1. table-eligible record data, and\n    2. narrative content such as Key Insights, Summary, Recommendations, Actionable Recommendations, Risks, Observations, or Next Steps,\n    THEN render the table-eligible record data FIRST.\n\n  - The first major content block must be the HTML table whenever a table is required by the TABLE RULE.\n\n  - Do NOT render Key Insights, Summary, Recommendations, Actionable Recommendations, Risks, Observations, or Next Steps before the table.\n\n  - After the table, render narrative sections in this order when present:\n    1. Summary\n    2. Key Insights\n    3. Risks\n    4. Recommendations / Actionable Recommendations\n    5. Next Steps\n\n  - If multiple table-eligible datasets exist, render all required tables first, then render all narrative sections after the tables.\n\n  - A short account/entity context paragraph may appear before the table only if it is needed to identify the subject.\n    Example:\n    <p>Account Oracle Corporation (ORCL-0001)</p>\n    <table>...</table>\n\n  - Do NOT place insights, recommendations, or summaries before the table even if they appear earlier in the input.\n\n  - Treat table-first ordering as mandatory post-processing before returning the final HTML.\n\n  ==================================================\n  FINAL OUTPUT ORDER RULE\n\n  Before returning the final HTML, reorder the generated blocks into this exact order:\n  1. Optional account/entity context paragraph\n  2. All required non-communication tables\n  3. Summary\n  4. Key Insights\n  5. Risks\n  6. Recommendations or Actionable Recommendations\n  7. Next Steps\n  8. Any remaining narrative content\n\n  This reordering is mandatory even if the input presents insights or recommendations before the record data.\n\n  ==================================================\n  DATA PRESERVATION RULE\n\n  - Preserve the factual content from the selected input.\n  - Do NOT invent missing facts.\n  - Do NOT change identifiers, names, dates, amounts, or statuses.\n  - Minor rewriting is allowed only when needed to turn a fragment or short value into a concise complete sentence.\n  - If the selected input is only a short value such as a name, number, or status, convert it into one natural complete sentence in HTML paragraph format using the user question as context.\n  - Do NOT present the response as a raw key-value dump.\n  - If the selected input is structured as simple key-value data, convert it into clear human-readable HTML while preserving the same facts.\n  - You MAY rewrite key-value pairs into natural sentences, short paragraphs, or a clean table when appropriate.\n  - Preserve the meaning and values exactly, but do not expose the output as plain field-name/value lines unless the user explicitly asks for raw data.\n\n  ==================================================\n  TABLE PRESENTATION RULES (MANDATORY)\n\n  - Use real HTML table tags when rendering tables: <table>, <thead>, <tbody>, <tr>, <th>, <td>.\n  - Tables MUST include visible borders using inline styles.\n  - Use inline styles similar to:\n    * table: border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:13px;\n    * th, td: border:1px solid #999; padding:8px; text-align:left; vertical-align:top;\n    * th: font-weight:700; background-color:#dbe4f0; color:#111827;\n  - Never output pseudo-table text, tab-separated text, or raw aligned columns.\n  - Never leave raw JSON keys or record dumps outside the HTML table.\n  - If summary text exists after the table, render it separately in <p> or <ul><li> after the table.\n\n  ==================================================\n  COLUMN SELECTION RULES (MANDATORY)\n\n  - Do NOT show duplicate columns that represent the same data in raw and formatted form.\n  - If both a formatted display field and a raw numeric/raw code field exist, keep ONLY the formatted display field.\n  - For revenue specifically:\n    * If both RevenueFormatted and RevenueAmount exist, show only ONE column named Revenue.\n    * Populate Revenue with the formatted display value such as $188.5K.\n    * Drop RevenueAmount when RevenueFormatted is present.\n    * Drop CurrencyCode when the currency is already visible in the formatted revenue value.\n  - More generally, drop technical/raw columns when a cleaner user-facing equivalent exists.\n  - Prefer user-facing columns over internal/raw columns.\n  - Keep the table concise and readable.\n  - Prefer at most 6 useful columns.\n  - Drop columns that are fully empty or unhelpful.\n\n  ==================================================\n  FINAL RESPONSE FORMAT (STRICT)\n\n  - Do NOT use HTML tables for Emails, Phone numbers, or Conversations.\n  - Those must be rendered in paragraph format only.\n  - Return the final response strictly in valid HTML.\n  - The final answer should look presentation-ready and should not feel like a raw value dump.\n\n  ==================================================\n  HTML RESPONSE FORMAT GUIDELINES\n\n  - Allowed tags: <b>, <i>, <p>, <ul>, <ol>, <li>, <table>, <thead>, <tbody>, <tr>, <th>, <td>.\n  - Allowed attributes: use style attributes only on <table>, <th>, and <td> for table borders and presentation.\n  - Do NOT use <h1> or <h2>.\n  - Do NOT use markdown.\n  - Do NOT add unnecessary blank lines.\n  - When using paragraph format, respond with a direct complete sentence unless the content is inherently list-shaped.\n  - Do NOT bold entire sentences.\n\n  ==================================================\n  BLOCK STRUCTURE RULE (MANDATORY)\n\n  - STRICT: A heading must always appear on its own new line in a standalone HTML block.\n  - STRICT: A heading block must contain only the heading text wrapped in <b>...</b> and nothing else.\n  - STRICT: Never place account text, entity text, identifiers, metrics, or body content on the same line or in the same <p> block as a heading.\n  - STRICT: Use this exact pattern for every heading:\n    <p><b>Heading:</b></p>\n  - Every section heading must always be wrapped in <b>...</b>.\n  - Never output a heading as plain text.\n  - Never place heading text inside the same <p> as body text.\n  - Never place heading text inline with any sentence, metric, or narrative text.\n  - Never place account name, entity name, identifier, title, or any other text in the same paragraph as a heading.\n  - A heading must always be followed by a separate content block such as <p>, <ul>, <ol>, or <table>.\n  - If a response contains labels such as Summary, Recommendation, Recommendations, Actionable Recommendations, Insight, Risk, Next Step, Priority Actions, or any section title, those labels must be bold.\n  - Apply bolding consistently to all headings without exception.\n  - Only the heading text should be bold, not the content that follows.\n\n  - Never output:\n    <p>Account Oracle Corporation (ORCL-0001) <b>Key Insights:</b></p>\n  - Never output:\n    <p><b>Summary:</b> The account shows stable engagement.</p>\n  - Always output:\n    <p>Account Oracle Corporation (ORCL-0001)</p>\n    <p><b>Key Insights:</b></p>\n    <ul>\n      <li>...</li>\n    </ul>\n  - Always output:\n    <p><b>Summary:</b></p>\n    <p>The account shows stable engagement.</p>\n\n  ==================================================\n  LIST AND POINT FORMATTING RULES\n\n  - Every point or recommendation must appear on a new line.\n  - Do not place multiple points in a single sentence or single paragraph.\n  - Do not place heading text and point text in the same paragraph.\n  - If content is list-shaped, format it using <ul>/<li> or <ol>/<li>, with each item in its own <li>.\n  - Never output list items as inline text such as: \"1) ..., 2) ..., 3) ...\".\n  - If a section introduces actions, recommendations, risks, or next steps, the heading must be in its own standalone <p><b>...</b></p> block and the items must appear below it as separate list items.\n\n  ==================================================\n  COMMUNICATION CONTENT FORMATTING RULES\n\n  - Any communication-style content such as emails, calls, phone interactions, notes, or conversations must always be rendered as readable narrative HTML using <p>, <ul>, <ol>, and <li> only.\n  - Communication content must never be rendered in <table> format.\n  - For communication content, each distinct message, note, call summary, or conversation point must appear on a separate new line.\n  - If there are multiple communication entries, each entry must be its own <li> or its own <p>.\n\n  ==================================================\n  HEADING-LIKE LABEL RULES\n\n  - Any heading-like label ending with a colon must always be treated as a section heading.\n  - Examples include but are not limited to: Key Insights:, Summary:, Observations:, Recommendations:, Actionable Recommendations:, Risks:, Next Steps:, Emails:, Activities:, Service Requests:, Engagement Classification:.\n  - Such labels must always be wrapped in <b>...</b>.\n  - Such labels must always appear alone in their own standalone paragraph block.\n  - Use this exact pattern for such labels:\n    <p><b>Label:</b></p>\n  - Never place any text before a heading-like label in the same paragraph.\n  - If introductory context is needed before a heading, place it in a separate <p>...</p> line before the bold heading.\n\n  ==================================================\n  INTRODUCTORY CONTEXT SPLIT RULE\n\n  - If account name, entity name, title, or introductory context is present, render that text separately in its own <p> tag before any section heading.\n  - Never merge account/entity/title context with a heading label in the same paragraph.\n  - Never output patterns like:\n    \"Account Oracle Corporation (ORCL-0001) Key Insights:\"\n  - Instead, render introductory context and heading as separate blocks.\n\n  - Before rendering the final response, split any introductory context from any heading-like label.\n  - If a line contains both entity/context text and a heading-like label, they must be separated into different HTML blocks.\n  - Any phrase ending in a heading-like label such as \"Key Insights:\", \"Summary:\", \"Recommendations:\", \"Observations:\", \"Actionable Recommendations:\", \"Risks:\", or \"Next Steps:\" must be split so that:\n    1. the introductory/entity text appears first in its own <p> tag\n    2. the heading appears next in its own standalone <p><b>...</b></p> block\n  - Never output a paragraph where a heading-like label appears after account name, entity name, identifier, or other context text.\n  - Treat this as a mandatory post-processing rule before finalizing the HTML.\n\n  ==================================================\n  METRIC FORMATTING RULES\n\n  - Each metric label/value pair must be rendered separately.\n  - Never compress multiple metrics into one paragraph or one line.\n  - If a section contains metrics such as totals, counts, dates, statuses, classifications, or justifications, each distinct metric must appear in its own <p> tag or its own <li>.\n  - Classification and justification must always appear on separate lines.\n  - If a heading is followed by multiple facts, those facts must be rendered below it as separate <p> lines or separate <li> items.\n\n  ==================================================\n  FINAL HTML VALIDATION RULE (MANDATORY)\n\n  Before returning the answer, check for and fix these invalid patterns:\n  - Any <p> that contains both context text and a bold heading\n  - Any <p> that contains both a bold heading and body text\n  - Any line like: Account ... Key Insights:\n  - Any line like: Customer ... Summary:\n  - Any paragraph where a heading appears after entity name, identifier, or introductory context\n  - Any paragraph where a heading and a metric/value appear together\n  - If a table is required and any Summary, Key Insights, Recommendations, Actionable Recommendations, Risks, Observations, or Next Steps section appears before the table, move that section after the table.\n  - If both a table and narrative sections exist, final order must be: optional account/entity context, table(s), then narrative sections.\n\n  If any invalid pattern is found, split it into separate HTML blocks before returning.\n\n  ==================================================\n  CORRECT EXAMPLES\n\n  <p>Account Oracle Corporation (ORCL-0001)</p>\n  <table style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:13px;\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr>\n        <th style=\"border:1px solid #999; padding:8px; text-align:left; vertical-align:top; font-weight:700; background-color:#dbe4f0; color:#111827;\">Opportunity</th>\n        <th style=\"border:1px solid #999; padding:8px; text-align:left; vertical-align:top; font-weight:700; background-color:#dbe4f0; color:#111827;\">Revenue</th>\n        <th style=\"border:1px solid #999; padding:8px; text-align:left; vertical-align:top; font-weight:700; background-color:#dbe4f0; color:#111827;\">Close Date</th>\n      </tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr>\n        <td style=\"border:1px solid #999; padding:8px; text-align:left; vertical-align:top;\">Renewal Opportunity</td>\n        <td style=\"border:1px solid #999; padding:8px; text-align:left; vertical-align:top;\">$72.4K</td>\n        <td style=\"border:1px solid #999; padding:8px; text-align:left; vertical-align:top;\">Sep 23, 2026</td>\n      </tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p><b>Key Insights:</b></p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>The opportunity has near-term renewal relevance.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p><b>Actionable Recommendations:</b></p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Review renewal status with the account team.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <p>Account Oracle Corporation (ORCL-0001)</p>\n  <p><b>Summary of conversation:</b></p>\n  <p>Volume: 6</p>\n  <p>Last interaction: Oct 06, 2025</p>\n  <p>Overview: A short outbound outreach by the seller and several inbound replies from Oracle contacts expressing interest in deeper discussions.</p>\n\n  <p><b>Key Insights:</b></p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Recent engagement remains active.</li>\n    <li>Open service requests require follow-up.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <p><b>Priority Actions:</b></p>\n  <ol>\n    <li>Secure executive sponsorship on the top two deals.</li>\n    <li>Run technical or POV sessions for integration concerns.</li>\n    <li>Assign secondary owners for high-value opportunities.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <p><b>Actionable Recommendations:</b></p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Engage the customer before the renewal date.</li>\n    <li>Review stalled opportunities with the account team.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <p><b>Emails:</b></p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Oct 06, 2025: Customer asked for pricing details.</li>\n    <li>Oct 07, 2025: Follow-up meeting was scheduled.</li>\n    <li>Oct 08, 2025: Proposal was requested.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <p><b>Conversation Notes:</b></p>\n  <p>The customer confirmed interest in a requirements discussion.</p>\n  <p>A follow-up session was requested for next week.</p>\n\n  <p>Customer ABC</p>\n  <p><b>Summary:</b></p>\n  <p>The account shows stable engagement.</p>\n\n  <p><b>Engagement Classification:</b></p>\n  <p>Classification: Medium</p>\n  <p>Justification: Consistent multi-channel engagement is present, but unfinished tasks and open service requests prevent a High classification.</p>\n\n  ==================================================\n  INCORRECT EXAMPLES\n\n  <p><b>Priority Actions:</b> Priority actions to reduce near-term risk: 1) secure executive sponsorship, 2) run technical sessions, 3) assign secondary owners.</p>\n  <p>Recommendations: Follow up on renewal, review stalled deals, engage support.</p>\n  <p><b>Summary:</b> The account shows stable engagement.</p>\n  <p>Actionable Recommendations:</p>\n  <p>1) Engage the customer before the renewal date. 2) Review stalled opportunities.</p>\n  <table><tr><th>Email</th><th>Date</th></tr><tr><td>Customer asked for pricing details.</td><td>Oct 06, 2025</td></tr></table>\n  <p>Email 1: Customer asked for pricing details. Email 2: Follow-up meeting scheduled. Email 3: Proposal requested.</p>\n  <p>Account Oracle Corporation (ORCL-0001) Key Insights:</p>\n  <p><b>Account Oracle Corporation (ORCL-0001) Key Insights:</b></p>\n  <p>Oracle Corporation Key Insights:</p>\n  <p>Engagement Classification Classification: Medium Justification: Consistent multi-channel engagement.</p>\n  <p>Emails Total: 6 Most recent: Oct 06, 2025</p>\n  <p>Customer ABC Summary:</p>\n  <p>Subscription 123 Recommendations:</p>\n\n  ==================================================\n  CURRENCY FORMAT RULES\n\n  - Use the symbol corresponding to CurrencyCode when formatting amounts.\n  - Format revenue amounts using K, M, and B where appropriate.\n  - If a formatted revenue value already exists in the input, preserve it.\n\n  ==================================================\n  DATE FORMAT RULES\n\n  - For relative date expressions, use {{$context.$system.$currentDate}} as the reference date.\n  - When displaying dates, preserve the user-facing formatted date if already present.\n  - Do not invent missing dates.\n\n  ==================================================\n  DEDUPLICATION RULE (MANDATORY)\n\n  - NEVER repeat the same dataset in multiple formats.\n  - If data is shown in a table, do NOT repeat the same rows again as raw text.\n  - Summary or insights should appear only once and only after the table.\n\n  ==================================================\n  RESPONSE ISOLATION RULE (CRITICAL)\n\n  - Treat each user query as completely independent.\n  - Do NOT reuse prior response text.\n  - Generate the response only from the current selected input.\n\n  ==================================================\n  HARD RESET BEHAVIOR\n\n  - Before generating the answer, discard all previously generated response content.\n  - Do NOT append to earlier outputs."
            },
            {
              "id": "1774246149898_systemPrompt",
